  "full_response": "Graph-based data modeling refers to the representation of data using graph structures, consisting of nodes (also called vertices) and edges (also called links) to depict real-world relationships and entities. This modeling approach is particularly useful for representing and analyzing connected data, where entities are interconnected in complex ways.\n\nGraph databases store data natively in the form of graphs and are optimized for managing connected data. They enable efficient queries to answer complex questions about relationships between entities, providing high performance and scalability.\n\nYou should consider using a graph database when your data involves complex relationships, and you need to perform queries involving deep graph traversals, answering \"who belongs to whom?\", or exploring patterns/trends within your data. For instance, applications in social networks, recommendation systems, fraud detection, and network analytics can benefit significantly from graph-based data modeling.",
